  "enhanced_information": "Antithrombin III deficiency is primarily associated with an increased risk of thrombosis rather than hemorrhage, aligning with the consensus that it does not typically cause bleeding disorders. Vitamin K deficiency leads to impaired synthesis of clotting factors, resulting in an increased risk of bleeding, which is well-documented. Liver disease is known to decrease the production of clotting factors, also leading to an increased risk of hemorrhage. Thrombocytopenia, characterized by a reduced platelet count, is directly associated with an increased risk of bleeding. Therefore, the option that is NOT typically associated with hemorrhage is Antithrombin III deficiency."
